//! zgx: grapheme clustering, DEC mode 2027, as Ghostty does it.
//!
//! In the mode a character continuing the grapheme cluster before the cursor
//! (UAX #29) joins that cell rather than taking one of its own, so an emoji
//! ZWJ sequence, a flag or a skin-toned emoji is one character in one or two
//! cells. Its width is the first character's, made two by VS16 or by any
//! character that has width inside a cluster (a spacing mark, the second
//! regional indicator), and one by VS15. A variation selector after
//! something with no emoji presentation, or a zero-width character that
//! continues nothing, is dropped.

use unicode_segmentation::GraphemeCursor;
use unicode_width::UnicodeWidthChar;

/// VS15, text presentation.
pub(crate) const VS15: char = '\u{fe0e}';
/// VS16, emoji presentation.
pub(crate) const VS16: char = '\u{fe0f}';

/// The characters above U+00FF that can continue a cluster, whatever their
/// width (which unicode-width's versions tell differently): combining and
/// spacing marks, Indic conjunct consonants (after a virama), Hangul jamo,
/// regional indicators, emoji modifiers, and pictographs (after a ZWJ).
/// Left out: Hangul syllables (they continue only a leading jamo, old Hangul
/// written in parts), CJK, kana and fullwidth forms (among them four
/// pictographs, 〰 〽 ㊗ ㊙, that continue a cluster after a ZWJ, as no emoji
/// sequence has them), what comes below U+0900 (what continues a cluster
/// there is zero-width, taken as such) and a character after a Prepend. Made from
/// `unicode-segmentation`; `the_continuing_table_is_what_segmentation_says`
/// checks it.
